{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "CodeSonar",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "LANG.ARITH.DIVZERO",
              "name": "Division By Zero",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Division By Zero"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The divisor of a division or modulo operation can be zero."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Dividing by zero is undefined behavior in C. Make sure the divisor is not zero before dividing, for example with an explicit check.",
                "markdown": "Dividing by zero is **undefined behavior** in C. Make sure the divisor is not zero before dividing, for example with an explicit check."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "LANG.STRUCT.RPL",
              "name": "Return Pointer To Local",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Return Pointer To Local"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A function returns the address of a local variable."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The lifetime of a local variable ends when the function returns, so the returned pointer is dangling. Return the value itself, use a caller-provided buffer, or allocate on the heap.",
                "markdown": "The lifetime of a local variable ends when the function returns, so the returned pointer is **dangling**. Return the value itself, use a caller-provided buffer, or allocate on the heap."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "IO.INJ.FMT",
              "name": "Format String Injection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Format String Injection"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Data that may be controlled by the user is used as a format string."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "If the format string contains conversion specifiers like %s or %n, printf reads or writes arbitrary memory. Always use a constant format string: printf(\"%s\", name).",
                "markdown": "If the format string contains conversion specifiers like `%s` or `%n`, `printf` reads or writes arbitrary memory. Always use a constant format string: `printf(\"%s\", name)`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "BADFUNC.GETS",
              "name": "Use of gets",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use of gets"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The function gets is used."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "gets cannot limit the number of characters it reads and always allows a buffer overrun. It was removed in C11. Use fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), stdin) instead.",
                "markdown": "`gets` cannot limit the number of characters it reads and always allows a **buffer overrun**. It was removed in C11. Use `fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), stdin)` instead."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ALLOC.UAF",
              "name": "Use After Free",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use After Free"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Memory is accessed after it has been freed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "After free, the memory may be reused for other data. Reading or writing it is undefined behavior and a common source of security vulnerabilities.",
                "markdown": "After `free`, the memory may be reused for other data. Reading or writing it is **undefined behavior** and a common source of security vulnerabilities."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ALLOC.DF",
              "name": "Double Free",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Double Free"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Memory is freed twice."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Freeing the same pointer twice corrupts the heap and can be exploited. Set pointers to NULL after freeing them or make sure each allocation is freed exactly once.",
                "markdown": "Freeing the same pointer twice corrupts the heap and can be exploited. Set pointers to `NULL` after freeing them or make sure each allocation is freed exactly once."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "LANG.STRUCT.CONDASSIG",
              "name": "Assignment In Conditional",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Assignment In Conditional"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An assignment is used as the condition of an if statement."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "This is often a typo for ==. If the assignment is intended, make it explicit: if ((x = parse_number(input)) != 0).",
                "markdown": "This is often a typo for `==`. If the assignment is intended, make it explicit: `if ((x = parse_number(input)) != 0)`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "LANG.STRUCT.EBS",
              "name": "Empty Loop Body",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Empty Loop Body"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A for loop has an empty body."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The semicolon directly after the loop header makes the loop body empty. This is often unintended. If it is intended, write an empty block {} to make that clear.",
                "markdown": "The semicolon directly after the loop header makes the loop body empty. This is often unintended. If it is intended, write an empty block `{}` to make that clear."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "LANG.MEM.NPD",
              "name": "Null Pointer Dereference",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Null Pointer Dereference"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A pointer that can be NULL is dereferenced."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Dereferencing a null pointer is undefined behavior and usually crashes the program. Check the pointer before use.",
                "markdown": "Dereferencing a null pointer is **undefined behavior** and usually crashes the program. Check the pointer before use."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ALLOC.LEAK",
              "name": "Leak",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Leak"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Allocated memory is not freed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Memory that is allocated but never freed is lost until the program exits. Free each allocation when it is no longer needed.",
                "markdown": "Memory that is allocated but never freed is lost until the program exits. Free each allocation when it is no longer needed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "LANG.STRUCT.GOTO",
              "name": "Use of goto",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Use of goto"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A goto statement is used."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "goto makes the control flow hard to follow. Prefer structured control flow such as loops, break and early returns.",
                "markdown": "`goto` makes the control flow hard to follow. Prefer structured control flow such as loops, `break` and early returns."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
